        <section class="intro">
            <p>
                Referral programs can generate revenue without relying entirely on paid advertising. But once customers can pool loyalty points, contribute rewards, and influence referrals, measuring the true return on investment becomes more complicated.
            </p>

            <p>
                A referral may generate an immediate purchase, a second purchase, and additional referrals later. If your reporting only counts the first transaction, you may underestimate the value of your referral program.
            </p>

            <p>
                This guide explains how to connect <strong>referral revenue attribution</strong> with <strong>ROI measurement</strong>, points pooling, customer contributions, email marketing, and long-term customer value.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="quick-answer">
            <h2>Quick Answer</h2>

            <p>
                Referral revenue attribution ROI measures how much financially attributable revenue your referral program produces compared with the total cost required to generate and manage that revenue.
            </p>

            <p>
                A useful process is:
            </p>

            <ol>
                <li>Track every referral source.</li>
                <li>Connect referred customers to their purchases.</li>
                <li>Measure revenue from the first purchase.</li>
                <li>Track repeat purchases when appropriate.</li>
                <li>Measure loyalty-point contributions and rewards.</li>
                <li>Calculate total referral-program costs.</li>
                <li>Compare attributed revenue with those costs.</li>
                <li>Optimize the program based on profitable customer segments.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

